{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1089","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1089","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1089\",\"parent_revision\":\"a4c23a9679f49315e494aeec1f57f09efbf943fd\",\"root_revision\":\"edd15de90cc363604482a4c5aa0d85bb3ddf8b24cb2ac148dea5a4231d6143c1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n**Current state**: Completed SAM tasks remain in plan files with full detail (requirements, constraints, acceptance criteria, verification steps). When an orchestrator runs `sam_status` or `sam_read` on a long-running plan with many completed tasks, the full task bodies consume context window tokens unnecessarily. No mechanism exists to summarize or compact completed work. File: `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/query.py` \u2014 all query functions return full task content regardless of completion status.\\n\\n**Target state**: A `sam_compact` CLI command and MCP tool that summarizes completed tasks in a plan file. For each COMPLETE task, the full body is replaced with a one-line summary preserving: task title, completion timestamp, key outcomes (files modified, tests added). The original full body is preserved in a `_compacted` metadata field or separate archive file for audit trail. `sam_status` output includes a `compacted_count` field showing how many tasks were compacted.\\n\\n**Measurable signal**: Run `uv run sam compact P{N}` on a plan with 5+ COMPLETE tasks. Before: `sam_status` output includes full task bodies for all tasks. After: completed tasks show summary-only in `sam_read` output, `compacted_count: N` appears in `sam_status`, and `sam_read --full` (or `--no-compact`) flag retrieves the original uncompacted body. Context token count for the plan drops measurably (verify by comparing character count of `sam_status` output before and after).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add semantic compaction for completed sam tasks to reduce context window consumption** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/task-management/beads.md \u2014 pattern: Semantic compaction (bd compact summarizes closed tasks using semantic compression)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-02)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 Context window consumption directly limits how many tasks an orchestrator can hold active. Plans with 10+ tasks (quality-gate plans are 6 tasks minimum) cause measurable context bloat during the later execution loop phases. P1 is appropriate: affects daily orchestrator efficiency.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Long-running plans (10+ tasks) where orchestrator reads `sam_status` or `sam_read` repeatedly, consuming growing context for already-complete work\\n- Bottleneck: `sam_read` (MCP) and `get_task_assignment` in `query.py` return full `Task.model_dump` with all body fields regardless of status \u2014 no filtering by completion\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Orchestrators executing plans with many completed tasks reclaim context window budget for active work\\n- `sam_status` gains observability over how much compaction has been applied (`compacted_count`)\\n- Completed task audit trail preserved via `_compacted` field \u2014 no data loss\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `sam compact P{N}` is run, each COMPLETE task's verbose body fields (description, requirements, constraints, acceptance_criteria, verification_steps, expected_outputs, context_notes, objective, handoff, body) are replaced with a one-line summary. The original content is preserved in a `_compacted` field or separate archive. `sam_status` output reports `compacted_count`. `sam_read` returns the compact summary by default; `sam_read --full` (or `--no-compact`) retrieves the original body.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nObservable target state:\\n- `uv run sam compact P{N}` exits 0 and reports N tasks compacted\\n- `sam_status P{N}` JSON includes a `compacted_count` integer field\\n- `sam_read P{N}/T{M}` for a compacted task returns a single-line summary, not the full body fields\\n- `sam_read P{N}/T{M} --full` returns the original uncompacted body fields\\n- The plan YAML file retains original content in a `_compacted` metadata field (or an archive sidecar file)\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `uv run sam compact P{N}` on a plan with 5+ COMPLETE tasks exits 0 and prints the count of tasks compacted\\n2. After compaction, `uv run sam status P{N} --format json` output includes `compacted_count: N` with correct value\\n3. After compaction, `uv run sam read P{N}/T{M}` for a COMPLETE task returns a one-line summary (not multi-field body) for the compacted task\\n4. `uv run sam read P{N}/T{M} --full` (or `--no-compact`) returns the original uncompacted body fields\\n5. The original body content is recoverable from the `_compacted` field in the plan YAML (or archive sidecar) \u2014 no data is silently discarded\\n6. NOT-STARTED and IN-PROGRESS tasks are unaffected by compaction\\n7. `sam_read` MCP tool honours the same compact-by-default / `--full` semantics via a `compact` boolean parameter (default True)\\n8. Before/after character-count comparison of `sam_status` output shows measurable reduction (at least 50% for plans where all tasks are complete)\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/query.py \u2014 `get_task_assignment`, `load_plan`, all query functions that return full Task content |\\n| Prior work | /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/models.py \u2014 `Task` model lines 172-196 (body fields); no `_compacted` field exists yet |\\n| Prior work | /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py \u2014 `sam_read` MCP tool (lines 100-134); `sam_status` MCP tool (lines 218-240); `sam_ready` compact routing manifest (lines 165-215) |\\n| Prior work | /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/cli.py \u2014 Typer `app` with existing subcommands; `compact` command does not exist yet |\\n| Prior work | /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/writers/yaml_writer.py \u2014 writer layer for YAML mutations |\\n| Research | /home/user/claude_skills/research/task-management/beads.md \u2014 `bd compact` pattern: semantic compression of closed tasks, preserves audit trail |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 standalone addition to sam_schema\\n- Blocks: None identified\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 Core changes touch: (1) `Task` model to add `_compacted` field, (2) `PlanStatus` model to add `compacted_count`, (3) `yaml_writer` to persist `_compacted` and strip body fields, (4) `query.py` to add `compact_plan()` function, (5) `server.py` to add `sam_compact` MCP tool and update `sam_read`/`sam_status`, (6) `cli.py` to add `sam compact` subcommand. No external dependencies. All within `sam_schema` package. Estimated 1\u20132 focused implementation sessions.\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- `Task` model in `models.py` has no `_compacted` field; adding it requires a model change and writer support. This is in-scope work, not a prerequisite blocker.\\n- `sam_status` `PlanStatus` model has no `compacted_count` field; same situation \u2014 in-scope addition.\\n\",\"digest\":\"b74209e847c5cf502e45b9b5ba8c3b9f0274b2331813c1f4a95ce0aee31dcb49\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg6VEg\"}"}